'''theophanic''' (thē-ō-fan′ik), ''a.'' [< ''theophan-y'' + ''-ic.''] Relating to a theophany; pertaining to an actual appearance of a god to man.

{{smaller block|The notion of angels as divine armies is not like that of the individual "messenger" closely connected with the ''theophanic'' history.
{{right|''W. R. Smith'', Encyc. Brit., II. 27.}}}}

'''theophany''' (thē-of′a̤-ni), ''n.'' [= OF. ''theophanie'', ''theophaine'', ''thiphanie'', ''thiphaine'', F. ''théophanie'' = OIt. ''theofania'', ''teofania'' = G. ''theophanie'', < ML. ''theophania'', ''theofania'', < Gr. θεοφάνεια, θεοφάνια, < θεός, god, + φαίνεσθαι, appear.] 1. A manifestation of God or of gods to man by actual appearance. {{smaller|The term is applied specifically to the appearance of God to the patriarchs in angelic or human form, and to Christ's nativity, baptism, and second coming.}}

{{smaller block|The Creator alone truly is; the universe is but a sublime ''theophany'', a visible manifestation of God.
{{right|''Milman'', Latin Christianity, viii. 5.}}}}

{{smaller block|The surest means of obtaining a knowledge of the [Homeric] gods, and of their will, was through their direct personal manifestation, in visible ''theophanies''.
{{right|''G. P. Fisher'', Begin. of Christianity, p. 84.}}}}

2. [''cap.''] The festival of the Epiphany.

'''theophilanthropic''' (thē-ō-fil-an-throp′ik), ''a.'' [< ''theophilanthrop-y'' + ''-ic.''] Of or pertaining to theophilanthropism or the theophilanthropists; uniting love to God with love to man.

{{smaller block|The ''theophilanthropic'' ideas of the Society for the Diffusion of Useful Knowledge.
{{right|''Contemporary Rev.'', XLIX. 341.}}}}

